hello there Steve Kaufman here  the linguist on language blogs I haven't  done a video in a while I've been  traveling I was in Europe for a couple  of weeks I got caught there by the ash  cloud as a consequence I had to drive  around from Stockholm to Hamburg from  Hamburg to Vienna but fortunately I was  able to get back on schedule I want to  talk today about grammar a subject that  comes up from time to time you know in  this whole discussion of language  learning we have the output versus input  debate and of course I'm very firmly on  the side of input as the place where I  like to spend most of my time when I'm  learning a language and that primarily  is because I find it effective and yet  very easy to organize although I must  admit now that I've been at my Russian  for a while I find myself wanting to do  more without put I have enough words now  I'm lining up more discussions that link  with our different Russian tutors when I  get my reports back with my mistakes I I  study them and they're fresh in my mind  because I've just finished trying to use  the language use it incorrectly so it  comes a point where you you you  definitely have to speak or write more  I'm too lazy to write but it's good for  it  but for a long while at least in my own  case I'm quite happy to rely on my input  activities to absorb the language now  about grammar where does that fit in now  on my blog the linguist not language  I've suggested that I'd like to do a  little poll amongst my readers to find  out where everyone stands on this issue  I sort of created two fictitious  positions that love the grammar lover  position in the grammar hater position  not love and hate are strong words I  mean the point is you know I want to  find out where people stand on the  importance of grammar in language study  in particular at the beginning  and I think a lot of people I'm part  surprised actually a lot of the people  who have commented that my blog are very  happy with grammar they like grammar  they like the terminology they find it  challenging interesting logical whatever  I don't and but everyone is different  I'm curious to know what what what the  majority of people believe I guess first  of all I have my own reaction to grammar  and and so I'll talk a little bit about  that and then I'll talk about what I  think are the way the ways in which we  learn language at least my understanding  of how the brain works and and try to  justify my somewhat anti grammar  position and in fact we may even have a  debate over at my blog I've asked people  to come forward and and have a debate  with me Vince has come forward but he  doesn't disagree with me that much so  we'll see how how strong a debate we  have I like debate  I like this agreement I think that  debate forces us to think about things  to justify our own positions and also to  listen to contrasting positions not  necessarily with the objective of  arriving at an agreement but simply to  stimulate ourselves and to stimulate  anyone who's listening grammar first of  all I have this tremendous aversion to  unnecessary terminology though even I  get in Russian for example I still don't  understand perfective and imperfective  verbs I understand that the idea of  aspect in other words that depending on  whether something is done regularly or  all the time or continuously or on the  other hand if it's a completed action  you'll have two different forms of the  verb however I have still a lot of  trouble figuring out whether in this  particular instance this is a completed  action or a continuous action and trying  to do it on the fly while speaking I  find extremely difficult and I've read  explanations and they make no impact on  me whatsoever  similarly with the cases there are so  many prepositions in Russia that take  the genitive for no other reason I mean  genitive means of something and yet  probably more prepositions take the  genitive than any other case all the  explanations make no sense some of the  explanations some of the weirdest things  are easiest to understand or at least to  remember like one year takes the  nominative two three four years takes  the genitive singular five and more  takes the genitive plural it's weird but  it's kind of straightforward but there's  so many other cases where are examples  of structures that have evolved a  certain way in a language and all of the  explanations and rules and terminology  don't help much I learned Japanese and  Chinese and I remember in those books  great fixed books de Francis and others  they invented a bunch of new terminology  for Chinese and Japanese I just ignored  it it didn't help me at all all I needed  to know was this is how they say things  in Chinese this is the pattern a few  examples not too many references to  exceptions and I'm off and then I  started looking for it and I see it and  when I see it I try and pay attention  much as I do now in link I will often  save phrases that embody the kinds of  structures that I have trouble with and  so I don't need in my own case you know  I've said that the language is like a  jigsaw puzzle and I just want to get out  the jigsaw puzzle putting the pieces  together and and I don't need a manual  of instructions on the jigsaw puzzle  there are certain basic concepts I guess  at the beginning of a language like this  here the verb comes at the end or at the  beginning or whatever but a lot of the  even for example the idea si in Spanish  that you don't always have to use for  the the the personal pronoun  you start to notice that after a while  and and I do admit that I will sometimes  refer to a grammar book not so much  looking things up but but sort of  leafing through it to see if there's  anything there that that might trigger  something make me more attentive to the  language but I find these terms like  modal's gerron's  I don't know in Korean as I say they  talk about copulative verbs all of this  terminology I am very very allergic to I  just I just resisted and the  explanations my eyes just glaze over but  a lot of the people who commented that  my blog they love like a grammar I think  personally that our brain works best on  all the reading that I've done suggests  that our brain works best at absorbing a  lot of input a lot of experience  stimulus and gradually forming its own  rules that that is more effective than  theoretical explanation of the rules if  I see someone doing something that's  more effective than a theoretical  explanation of how I should do that but  others I gather like grammar is it a  function of the fact that many of the  readers of my blog are linguists in the  sense of having studied linguistics I  don't know I call myself a linguist in  the first sense of the word that I find  in dictionary definitions and that is as  someone who speaks more than one  language but obviously people who have  invested a lot of time and effort into  learning a lot of this terminology once  they've learned it that they find it  useful to apply to each successive  language that they learn yeah I don't I  don't see it and you know the idea that  that the grammar is useful whether we  like it or not someone mentioned  my blog well if we don't like it it's  not useful because when you're learning  something your emotional reaction to  what you're learning whether you like it  or not like whether you like the  language or not whether you like the  content or not whether you like a  certain learning strategy or not all of  these things are very subjective and  your emotional reaction to these things  will influence how useful these things  are and in my case I find grammar not so  useful that's not that I haven't tried I  have studied I have pored over tables  you know I have tried to understand  these explanations of imperfect and  perfect in Ferb's they just don't  penetrate my scalp but I do believe that  with enough exposure paying attention  enjoying the language certain patterns  start slowly to become more and more  natural and that's my take on it you can  also read up the recent thread at the  linguist on language and thank you for  listening bye for now
 

